(Bug #100: 0.4.10 rendered `{n:0}` and `{n:00000}` as width 0, which pads
 nothing - the manual's width row has no floor on N, and 0.4.10 agreed.
 0.4.11's #98 fix read the zero-pad branch left to right, consuming the
 leading `0` and then demanding digits after it; a clause of nothing BUT
 zeros left no digits behind, fell into #98's new catch-all, and was
 reported as an unknown specifier - a regression the fuzzer's own CI
 caught on the 0.4.11 re-pin. Josj's ruling (2026-08-23): width 0 is a
 legal no-op, same as any width too small to add characters.

 A width asks nothing of a type (LANGUAGE.md, the width rule), so the
 no-op holds for a float and a text exactly as it does for a number - none
 of them pad. `{n:04x}` is unaffected on purpose: it has a nonzero width
 (4) before the base letter, so it never reaches the all-zeros branch.)

a number called n is 42.
a float called f is 3.5.
a text called t is "hi".

(The bug's own repro shape: bare zero and all-zeros, both a no-op.)
Print "[{n:0}]".
Print "[{n:00000}]".

(A width of zero on the other two types - same no-op, nothing padded.)
Print "[{f:0}]".
Print "[{t:0}]".

(Regression: a real zero-padded width before a base letter must not move.)
Print "[{n:04x}]".
